{"name":"Rosewood Brown","hex":"#72371c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rosewood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The fragrant wood of Dalbergia nigra, a Brazilian tree in the legume family, which has a sweet smell.","Any of several dozen woods, resembling that of Dalbergia nigra in some respect."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rosewood"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rosewood","blocks":[{"text":"Rosewood is any of a number of richly hued hardwoods, often brownish with darker veining, but found in other colours. It is hard, tough, strong, and dense. True rosewoods come from trees of the genus Dalbergia, but other woods are often called rosewood. Rosewood takes a high polish and is used for luxury furniture-making, flooring, musical instruments, and turnery."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Rio-Palisander%2C_dunkel_Holz.JPG/330px-Rio-Palisander%2C_dunkel_Holz.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosewood","wikidata":"Q122421"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"},{"title":"Shades of red","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color red may differ in hue, chroma, lig